          </Node>
          <Pad Id="LZD5Qfr6FwOPjMytoZxwH4" Bounds="996,89,537,415" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="ValueMap and ColorMap&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The ValueMap and ColorMap nodes are particularly important, as they allow textures to drive parameters and effects throughout a patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ValueMap takes a texture as input and returns a numerical value. You can think of it as mapping each pixel of a texture to a single number. This is useful for controlling parameters such as size, opacity, speed, or position.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ColorMap also takes a texture as input, but returns an RGBA colour value, which can be converted into a Vector4. You can think of it as mapping each pixel of a texture to a four-dimensional vector containing the Red, Green, Blue, and Alpha channels.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Both nodes make it possible to use textures as data sources rather than simply as images. In practice, this means that whenever you encounter a pin expecting a GPU&lt;Vector4&gt;, you can often drive it using a ColorMap connected to a texture. Likewise, whenever a pin expects a GPU&lt;float&gt;, a ValueMap can often be used to provide the required data.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This approach is extremely powerful because it allows images, gradients, procedural textures, and animated patterns to control complex visual effects directly on the GPU.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="VYoUBLYrZszMQEb43MfsJn" Bounds="1001,510,531,454" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Texture FX in Stride&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Stride provides a large collection of texture generation and processing nodes that are both powerful and flexible. By combining these nodes, you can create anything from simple gradients and masks to complex procedural textures and animated effects.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The best way to explore their capabilities is to study the example patches included with vvvv gamma. These demonstrate common workflows and show how multiple texture effects can be chained together to create more sophisticated results.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In addition to the built-in nodes, many more texture-related nodes are available through the VL.Addons package:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;VL.Addons Package - https://www.nuget.org/packages/VL.Addons&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The package extends the standard node library with additional texture generators, filters, and utility nodes, making it a valuable resource once you are familiar with the basics.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
